   **Location:** `ets2panda/ir/ets/etsTypeReferencePart.cpp:174` and `ets2panda/checker/types/ets/etsObjectType.cpp:1480`  
   **Spec basis:** `3_types.rst` Partial Utility Type says `Partial<T>` contains all fields/properties of `T` as optional; `9_classes.rst` says class members include members inherited from the direct superclass; `5_generics.rst` says different generic instantiations are distinct unless variance applies.  
   **Explanation:** The previous one-level case (`Derived<T> extends Base<T>`) appears addressed, but the fix still only looks/copies one superclass level. This still fails when inherited generic fields are separated by multiple empty subclasses, e.g.:
   ```ets
   class A<T> { value: T | undefined }
   class B<T> extends A<T> {}
   class C<T> extends B<T> {}
   class D<T> extends C<T> {}

   let p: Partial<D<string>> = {}
   p.value = "x" // should be string|undefined
   ```
   `HandlePartialType` only checks `originalType` and `originalType->SuperType()` for fields, so if both are empty it skips substitution even though an ancestor has fields. `CopyPropertyGroupWithBase` also only copies from `base` and `base->SuperType()`, so it can miss fields beyond one empty superclass. The added multilevel tests do not cover this because each intermediate level declares its own field.  
   **Suggested fix:** Traverse the full superclass chain when deciding whether substitution is needed and when copying inherited fields, or avoid field-existence gating and instantiate based on the generic original type while handling recursion explicitly. Add a test with two or more empty intermediate subclasses above the field-bearing base.
